# Agent Behavior Rules

## CLI-First Mandate
- **ALWAYS use CLI tools instead of telling the user to open Android Studio**
- Use `./gradlew` for builds, tests, lint, dependency resolution, and Firebase / Play Console publishing
- Use `adb` for installs, launches, screenshots, system settings, nav-mode toggles, logcat, and `dumpsys` introspection
- Use the `emulator` CLI to boot AVDs (`emulator -avd <name>`)
- Use `sdkmanager` / `avdmanager` for SDK installs and AVD creation
- Use `scrcpy` to mirror the device into a normal window the user can show on screen
- Only mention the Android Studio GUI when the task genuinely requires it (interactive debugger / step-through, real-time Layout Inspector overlays on a running app, Profiler flamegraph UI)

## When Android Studio GUI Is Unavoidable
- Don't just say "open Android Studio" — provide **step-by-step instructions** with exact menu paths, tool window names, and what to look for
- Example: "In Android Studio, open the `Run` menu → `Debug 'app'`, set a line breakpoint by clicking the gutter next to line 42 of `BaseActivity.kt`, then in the Debug tool window (bottom panel, bug icon) use the `Variables` pane to inspect..."
- Assume the user has minimal Android Studio experience

## Required environment (export on EVERY shell command)
The user's default shell does **not** have these set. Prepend them to every
build / `adb` / `emulator` / `sdkmanager` command, or run them once at the top
of each persistent shell:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME="/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home"
export ANDROID_HOME=~/Library/Android/sdk
export PATH="$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$ANDROID_HOME/emulator:$PATH"
```

If `JAVA_HOME` is unset, Gradle does **not** fail with a clean "JAVA_HOME is
not set" message — it silently falls back to whatever `java` is on `PATH`
(usually a system Java 8 JRE), and the build fails much later with a cryptic
variant-matching wall of text. **Recognize these symptoms as JAVA_HOME-not-set
in disguise:**

- `Could not resolve com.android.tools.build:gradle:X.Y.Z` (or any plugin)
- `No matching variant of <plugin> ... was found`
- `Incompatible because this component declares a component ... compatible with Java 11 [or 17]` paired with `the consumer needed a component ... compatible with Java 8`
- `adb: command not found` / `emulator: command not found`
- `SDK location not found` (this one is `ANDROID_HOME`, same family of issue)

When you see any of those, the recovery is always:

```bash
./gradlew --stop      # kill stale Java-8 daemons spawned before the env fix
export JAVA_HOME="/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home"
export ANDROID_HOME=~/Library/Android/sdk
export PATH="$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$ANDROID_HOME/emulator:$PATH"
# then retry the original command
```

The `./gradlew --stop` matters: Gradle reuses daemon processes across runs,
and a daemon started under Java 8 stays on Java 8 even after you fix
`JAVA_HOME` — you have to kill it first.

## Search Before You Write
- Always search existing code before generating new files or patterns
- Check `app/src/main/java/com/mdrt/mdrtacademy/networking/` for HTTP / API endpoints (`NetworkingService.kt`, `NetworkingModule.kt`)
- Check `app/src/main/java/com/mdrt/mdrtacademy/model/` for existing model definitions (mix of Kotlin and Java)
- Check `app/src/main/java/com/mdrt/mdrtacademy/util/Constants.kt` for existing identifiers, URLs, keys
- Check `app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml` for existing string keys (do **not** hardcode user-facing strings in Kotlin)
- Check `app/src/main/java/com/mdrt/mdrtacademy/activity/` and `app/src/main/java/com/mdrt/mdrtacademy/ui/` for existing Activities / Fragments before creating new ones (`BaseActivity.kt` is the root extension point)
- Check `app/build.gradle` for the dependency list before adding a new library

## Build Verification
- After making Kotlin / Java / XML changes, verify the build compiles:
  ```bash
  cd MDRTAcademy && ./gradlew :app:assembleQaDebug 2>&1 | tail -30
  ```
- After updating layout XML or drawable resources, the build is the only signal — there's no separate validator
- After dependency changes in `app/build.gradle`, run `./gradlew :app:dependencies | head -50` to confirm the resolution graph
- For Firebase QA distribution, the verified one-liner is `./gradlew :app:assembleQaRelease :app:appDistributionUploadQaRelease`
- For Play Console production DRAFT upload, the verified one-liner is `./gradlew :app:publishProductionReleaseBundle`

## Release request behavior (Firebase QA, Play Console production)

When the user asks for a release — any of: *"ship to QA"*, *"release to
Firebase"*, *"distribute to testers"*, *"publish to Play Store"*, *"send to
production"*, *"deploy"*, or similar — **do not just execute it**. Pause and
present these three options first using Cursor's **interactive multiple-choice
UI** (the structured single-select question picker) — **NOT** as plain text or
a markdown blockquote. Rendering them as selectable options is what lets the
user click a choice; presenting them as prose is a known regression (and
diverges from the sibling iOS rule, which already mandates the interactive
picker — keep the two platforms in lockstep).

Ask **one single-select question**, prompt it with **"Which one?"** (a title
like *"How should I run this release?"* is good), and give it exactly these
three options. List option 1 **first** and append **"(Recommended)"** to its
label, per the picker's convention:

1. **I run it for you** — I invoke the Gradle task here, you watch the output
   stream in this chat, I report the result (task name, versionCode burned, the
   URL of the new release, what's left for a human). Recommended for routine
   releases.
2. **I give you the command** — I show you the exact command + any prerequisites
   to check first, you paste and run in your own terminal. I'll verify the
   result with you when you tell me it finished. Recommended if you want to keep
   your hands on the wheel.
3. **You do it through the Console UI** — I walk you step-by-step through
   Firebase Console or Play Console with exact menu paths, what to upload, what
   to fill in. Recommended if something with the CLI is broken or you want to
   verify the release visually before it goes out.

The picker supplies a free-text "Other" escape hatch automatically, so don't add
one. Do **not** also restate the options as a bulleted/numbered list in the
message body — the picker IS the menu.

Then respond based on the choice:

### If option 1 — "I run it for you"
- Confirm prerequisites first: credential files present (`firebase-service-account.json` or `mdrtacademy-qa-*.json`), working tree state (any uncommitted changes that'll go into the build?), current `versionCode` if production.
- Run the appropriate Gradle one-liner:
  - Firebase QA: `./gradlew :app:assembleQaRelease :app:appDistributionUploadQaRelease --console=plain`
  - Firebase Preprod: `./gradlew :app:assemblePreproductionRelease :app:appDistributionUploadPreproductionRelease --console=plain`
  - Play Console production DRAFT: `./gradlew :app:publishProductionReleaseBundle --console=plain`
- Stream output. After completion, report: exact task(s) invoked, `versionCode` burned (production only), distribution target (group alias for Firebase / track for Play), URL of the new release, what's left for a human (e.g. "Send for review" button in Play Console).

### If option 2 — "I give you the command"
- Show the full command with env-export prefix so it's copy-paste safe in any shell:
  ```bash
  export JAVA_HOME="/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home" \
    && export ANDROID_HOME=~/Library/Android/sdk \
    && export PATH="$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$ANDROID_HOME/emulator:$PATH" \
    && cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/MDRTAcademy" \
    && ./gradlew <task-here> --console=plain
  ```
- List prerequisites to check first (credentials, branch, versionCode).
- Tell them what success looks like in the output (`BUILD SUCCESSFUL`, the upload task line, the URL the task should print).
- Offer: "When you're done, paste the last 20 lines of output and I'll verify."

### If option 3 — "You do it through the Console UI"
- For Firebase App Distribution:
  1. Open Firebase Console → select the project that owns the QA Android app
  2. Left sidebar → **App Distribution**
  3. Top tabs → **Releases** → click **Upload new release**
  4. Drag in the APK (build it locally first if needed: `./gradlew :app:assembleQaRelease`, find at `app/build/outputs/apk/qa/release/app-qa-release.apk`)
  5. Paste release notes (or upload the contents of `MDRTAcademy/release_notes.txt`)
  6. In **Testers and groups** field, type the group alias (e.g. `LPL+MDRT`)
  7. Click **Distribute**
- For Play Console production DRAFT:
  1. Open Play Console → select the production app
  2. Left sidebar → **Production** (under "Release" group)
  3. Top tabs → **Releases** → click **Create new release**
  4. Upload the AAB (build it locally first if needed: `./gradlew :app:bundleProductionRelease`, find at `app/build/outputs/bundle/productionRelease/app-production-release.aab`)
  5. Set release name (default suggested: matches `versionName` from `app/build.gradle`)
  6. Paste release notes per locale
  7. **Important:** click **Save as draft**, not **Review release**, to match the `releaseStatus.DRAFT` guard in the codebase
  8. Confirm the draft is visible in the Production track's Releases tab
- After the user reports done, offer to verify (e.g. "want me to check the Play Console API for the draft's status?").

### Exception: never skip the menu

Even if the user's prompt looks unambiguous (*"just run the prod release"*), present the three options first **through the interactive picker** (never as a prose menu, even when the prompt already spells out which option they want). The user can click option 1 immediately — it's a 5-second beat, not a blocker — but the picker enforces a deliberate-choice gate that's appropriate for irreversible operations like burning a `versionCode` or pushing to a real Play Store listing.

## Onboarding new developers / bootstrapping new projects

Two companion rules cover setup scenarios. Both load on demand; you don't
need to memorize their contents — just route the user to the right one and
follow its sequence.

- **Joining the existing team** (Firebase + Play Console already configured,
  user just needs local toolchain + credentials): point them at
  `.cursor/rules/android-project-onboarding.mdc`. Triggers on phrases like
  *"how do I set this up"*, *"first time on this repo"*, *"can't build"*,
  *"what do I need to install"*, *"onboard me"*.
- **Bootstrapping a brand-new Android project** (no Firebase yet, no Play
  Console listing yet, needs the plugins wired from scratch): point them at
  `.cursor/rules/android-project-bootstrap.mdc`. Triggers on phrases like
  *"new Android project"*, *"setting up Firebase"*, *"setting up Play
  Console"*, *"first time publishing"*, *"replicate this delivery setup in
  another repo"*. **Warn the user up front** that this is an afternoon of
  hands-on work plus 1–2 days of Play developer-account verification waits,
  and there's a $25 Play Console fee.

If you're unsure which case applies, ask the user one question: *"Are you
joining an existing team's project that already has Firebase + Play set up,
or are you starting from a blank Android project with no Firebase or Play
yet?"*

## Delivery pipeline guardrails
- **Production publishes are wired as `releaseStatus.DRAFT`** in `app/build.gradle`, inside the `playConfigs.production` block. Find it with `grep -n "playConfigs" MDRTAcademy/app/build.gradle` (line number drifts with edits; do not hardcode it). This means an upload creates a Play Console DRAFT but does **not** submit for Google review — a human still clicks "Send for review" in the Play Console UI. **Never** change this to `COMPLETED` without explicit user permission. **Verify the wiring actually exists on the current branch before promising a production publish:** the `playConfigs.production` block, the `com.github.triplet.play` plugin in the `plugins {}` block, and the `com.github.triplet.gradle:play-publisher` classpath in the root `build.gradle` all need to be present. If any are missing, the agent is on a branch that doesn't have delivery wired up — say so and stop; do **not** improvise the wiring (the triplet plugin's default `releaseStatus` is `COMPLETED`, which auto-publishes).
- Every uploaded AAB burns a `versionCode` forever, even discarded drafts. The triplet `play-publisher` plugin has `resolutionStrategy.AUTO` set, so if a `versionCode` collision happens it auto-bumps — don't manually bump `versionCode` unless that fails too.
- Required credential files (all gitignored):
  - `firebase-service-account.json` — Firebase App Distribution upload
  - `google-services.json` — Firebase client config
  - `mdrtacademy-qa-1db5bc700e4e.json` — Google Play API service account
  - `keystores/mdrtacadedmy-release` + `keystore.properties` — release signing
- If any of those are missing, **stop and tell the user** rather than improvising — the build will fail in a confusing way mid-pipeline otherwise.

