## Booting the emulator (do this first; it has sharp edges)

The AVD is `Medium_Phone_API_36` (API 36, arm64, Play Store image). A naive
`emulator -avd ... &` from an agent shell does **not** work reliably — use the
committed launcher AND the post-boot network kick:

```bash
python3 MDRTAcademy/scripts/agent-boot-emulator.py        # detaches + cold-boots
export PATH="$HOME/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools:$PATH"
adb wait-for-device
until [ "$(adb shell getprop sys.boot_completed | tr -d '\r')" = "1" ]; do sleep 2; done

# Post-boot network kick — REQUIRED on every cold boot, not just wipe-data.
# Without it, ConnectivityService reports the default route as installed but
# the Linux kernel routing table is missing it ("Network is unreachable" for
# any host outside 10.0.2.0/24, ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED in WebViews, etc.).
# See the ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED gotcha below for the why.
adb shell cmd connectivity airplane-mode enable
sleep 3
adb shell cmd connectivity airplane-mode disable
sleep 8
```

A clean cold boot + network kick is ~30s (boot ~15-20s, kick ~12s). The launcher exists because two failure modes
bite a plain background launch (both documented in the script header):

1. **Process-group teardown.** When you background the emulator and the
   spawning agent shell returns, the harness tears down the process group and
   the emulator dies a second or two later ("boots, then the window vanishes").
   macOS has no `setsid`, so the launcher daemonizes with a double-fork +
   `os.setsid()` to fully detach. `nohup`/`disown` alone is **not** enough.
2. **Corrupt Quick Boot snapshot.** A stale `default_boot` snapshot can fail
   GPU restore (`ERROR | Failed to find ColorBuffer:0`) and crash qemu right
   after the window appears. The launcher always passes `-no-snapshot-load`,
   and the AVD's `config.ini` is set to `fastboot.forceColdBoot = yes` so even
   a plain launch is safe. If you ever see the ColorBuffer crash again, delete
   `~/.android/avd/Medium_Phone.avd/snapshots/default_boot` (the AVD's data
   dir is `Medium_Phone.avd`, not `Medium_Phone_API_36.avd`).

The launcher also uses software GPU (`-gpu swiftshader_indirect`) for headless
reliability and `-partition-size 16384` so the AVD's `/data` is 16 GB, big
enough that the next ~128 MB APK install never hits
`INSTALL_FAILED_INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE`. To shut the emulator down cleanly:
`adb emu kill`.

**Storage discipline (read before debugging an INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE failure):**

The naive intuition — "old version is still installed, just uninstall it" — is
almost always wrong on this AVD. **Most of `/data` is system bloat** (Play
Services updates, OEM image updates, dalvik AOT cache) that `adb uninstall`
doesn't touch. On a default 6 GB partition, accumulated bloat can leave under
500 MB free even when zero third-party packages are installed. That's why
this project bumps the partition to 16 GB at AVD-create time AND launch time.

**Preflight before boot: size-check the APK against the partition.**

The 16 GB default handles most apps. For unusually large APKs, **bump the
partition BEFORE booting** rather than discovering the problem mid-install
when you've already spent a minute on cold boot. Real install footprint is
roughly 3-5× the APK size (extracted resources + dexopt + native libs +
install-time staging), so size the partition accordingly. If you have a
target APK on disk:

```bash
ls -lh /path/to/<your-build>.apk   # check size before booting
```

| APK size | Partition needed | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| < 200 MB | 16 GB (default) | Just boot. The launcher handles it. |
| 200 MB – 1 GB | 16 GB (default) | Just boot, but **preflight `df -h /data` before installing**. |
| 1 – 2 GB | 32 GB | Wipe + bump BEFORE booting (see procedure below). `-partition-size 32768`. |
| > 2 GB | 64+ GB | Wipe + bump BEFORE booting. `-partition-size 65536`. Note: image grows on disk too. |

If you don't know the APK size yet (haven't built it), default to 16 GB and
fall back to a resize later if the install fails. But if you've just been
asked to install a specific known-large APK, do the resize first — it costs
~30 seconds and saves you a failed-install + retry cycle.

**Preflight after boot, before any APK install ≥ 50 MB:**

```bash
adb shell df -h /data            # want >1 GB free; warn if <500 MB
```

**Permanent partition-resize procedure** (use this when the APK-size check
above tells you 16 GB isn't enough, or when an existing AVD still has the
old 6 GB layout):

1. `adb emu kill`
2. Edit `~/.android/avd/Medium_Phone.avd/config.ini` and set:
   `disk.dataPartition.size = <bytes>` — values: 16 GB = `17179869184`, 32 GB = `34359738368`, 64 GB = `68719476736`. *(The original default was 6442450944 = 6 GB.)*
3. Boot once with `emulator -avd Medium_Phone_API_36 -wipe-data -partition-size <MB> -no-snapshot-load` to recreate the image at the new size (16 GB = `16384`, 32 GB = `32768`, 64 GB = `65536`). Loses any cached state on the emulator (Google sign-in, dev settings).
4. Update the launcher script's `-partition-size` line to match, so future boots stay at the new size without needing `-wipe-data`.
5. **Apply the standard post-boot network kick** (same one in the "Booting the emulator" section — it's not special-purpose for wipe-data, it's required on every cold boot). If you skip it, the next network-touching operation fails with `Network is unreachable` / `ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED` / `UnknownHostException`. Verify after with `adb shell ping -c 1 8.8.8.8`.

`adb uninstall com.mdrt.mdrtacademy.qa` still helps marginally between dry-run
cycles, but it's a +100 MB recovery, not a fix.

## On-device interaction (the agent's "browser" for mobile)
- Install / update an APK: `adb install -r path/to/app-qa-debug.apk`
- Force-stop to reset state: `adb shell am force-stop com.mdrt.mdrtacademy.qa`
- Launch: `adb shell monkey -p com.mdrt.mdrtacademy.qa -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1`
- Identify topmost Activity: `adb shell dumpsys activity activities | grep topResumedActivity`
- Tap / swipe: `adb shell input tap <x> <y>` / `adb shell input swipe <x1> <y1> <x2> <y2> <duration_ms>` — **coordinates are device pixels matching the `screencap` output; see "Tap coordinate discipline" below**
- Get device resolution: `adb shell wm size` *(this AVD is `1080x2400`)*
- Screenshot (3-step idiom — capture, pull, clean up):
  ```bash
  adb shell screencap -p /sdcard/s.png \
    && adb pull /sdcard/s.png /tmp/<name>.png \
    && adb shell rm /sdcard/s.png
  ```
- Force 3-button nav (matches the most-reported bug scenario):
  ```bash
  adb shell cmd overlay enable com.android.internal.systemui.navbar.threebutton
  adb shell cmd overlay disable com.android.internal.systemui.navbar.gestural
  ```
- Restore gesture nav: swap the two `enable`/`disable` overlay names.

After taking a screenshot, **read it back into the chat as an artifact** so the user can see what the device actually rendered.

### Tap coordinate discipline (the #1 cause of "wrong-spot tap" iteration loops)

`adb shell input tap X Y` expects coordinates in the **device's native pixel
space** — the exact same coordinate system as the `screencap` output PNG.
This AVD is `1080x2400`. Confirm if you're unsure: `adb shell wm size`.

The recurring failure mode that wastes a turn:

1. Agent takes a screenshot
2. The chat UI may render the PNG at a different size than its actual pixel dimensions
3. Agent eyeballs coordinates from the visually-displayed image (or invents "scale" math) → coordinates end up at screenshot-display scale, not device-pixel scale
4. `input tap` lands hundreds of pixels off the intended target
5. Agent screenshots again, sees the miss, tries to "correct" — burns another turn

**The discipline:**

- Treat the **PNG file on disk** as the source of truth for coordinates. Open it directly (or trust the chat-rendered image only if you've confirmed the rendered size equals the file's pixel size).
- Coordinates you read from the PNG file are directly usable in `input tap` — no scaling, no normalization, no dp/sp conversion.
- **WebViews are not special.** WebView content renders at the device's pixel density, so coordinates inside a WebView use the same absolute device-pixel coordinate system as native UI. Don't translate "client X/Y"-style browser coordinates; use absolute screen pixels.
- If a tap misses on the first try, screencap and **verify your coordinate space before retrying** — don't iteratively guess. The most common silent failure: an agent reads coordinates from a 540×1200 thumbnail rendering and halves them for the actual `input tap` call. Both versions are wrong; only the raw 1080×2400 file coordinates are correct.

For elements where pixel-perfect tapping is fragile (small buttons near screen edges, dynamically-positioned elements), prefer one of:

- `adb shell input keyevent` for hardware keys (`KEYCODE_BACK`, `KEYCODE_HOME`, `KEYCODE_TAB`, etc.)
- `adb shell input text "string"` for text entry (works regardless of which field has focus, as long as one does)
- `adb shell am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d <deep-link>` to jump straight to an activity instead of navigating through taps

## User Context
- The primary developer is a **Ruby on Rails / web backend developer** with minimal Android / Kotlin experience
- Explain Android / Kotlin / Gradle concepts using Rails / web analogies when helpful
- Be explicit about where files go and why — don't assume familiarity with Android project conventions
- When explaining Android patterns, relate to MVC (which Rails devs know well):
  - `Activity` / `Fragment` = Rails Controller (with View merged in for screen-level UI)
  - Layout XML (`res/layout/*.xml`) = ERB / HTML view template (declarative, mostly inert)
  - `ViewModel` (AAC) = service object / presenter — survives config changes the way a server-side controller survives a single request
  - Data class / `data class Foo(...)` in `model/` = ActiveRecord model **without** DB persistence (no rows, just shape)
  - `NetworkingService` (Retrofit) = HTTP client / API wrapper (like Faraday / HTTParty wrappers)
  - `SharedPreferences` = session / cookies (small key-value, on-disk, app-scoped)
  - Room database = ActiveRecord with SQLite (DAOs ≈ scopes, `@Entity` ≈ a model class)
  - `LiveData` / `Flow` / EventBus = pub/sub / `ActiveSupport::Notifications`
  - `Intent` = a serialized route + params (you "navigate" by handing the OS an `Intent` the way you'd `redirect_to`)
  - Gradle = Bundler + Rake + Webpacker in one. `build.gradle` is `Gemfile` + `Rakefile` + asset pipeline config.
  - AndroidManifest.xml = `config/routes.rb` + `config/application.rb` + permissions declarations, fused into one XML file
  - `R.string.foo` lookups in code = `t('foo')` i18n lookups in Rails views

## Common gotchas (recognize these failure modes)
- **JAVA_HOME / ANDROID_HOME / PATH symptoms** → see the "Required environment" section above. This is the #1 cause of confusing Gradle / adb failures in this repo and the symptoms are non-obvious; check that section first before chasing anything else.
- **`Could not resolve all artifacts`** (NOT paired with a "compatible with Java 8" variant message) → genuine network issue or proxy. Retry; if it persists, check `gradle.properties` for `org.gradle.jvmargs` and proxy settings. (If you DO see the Java 8 variant message, it's `JAVA_HOME`, not the network.)
- **App installs but crashes on launch with `INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE`** → the signing certificate changed (you built a release variant over a debug, or vice versa). `adb uninstall <package_id>` then re-install.
- **`adb` reports `device offline`** → `adb kill-server && adb start-server && adb devices`. If still offline, the emulator process is wedged; kill and reboot it.
- **Emulator "boots for a second then closes"** → almost always one of the two boot failure modes in the "Booting the emulator" section: a corrupt Quick Boot snapshot (`Failed to find ColorBuffer:0` in the log) or process-group teardown of a backgrounded launch. Use `MDRTAcademy/scripts/agent-boot-emulator.py` rather than launching `emulator` directly.
- **`net::ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED` in WebViews / `UnknownHostException` in network calls** → almost always means the emulator has no network at all, not a DNS-specific issue. Verify with `adb shell ping -c 1 8.8.8.8` — if it reports "Network is unreachable", `dumpsys connectivity` will paradoxically show the default route as registered while the kernel's `ip route` is missing it. Root cause: every cold boot from `-no-snapshot-load` hits a `ConnectivityService`/kernel route-install desync. The documented boot procedure in the "Booting the emulator" section runs the airplane-mode toggle automatically after `wait-for-device`; if you're still seeing this symptom, the kick wasn't applied after the most recent boot. Run it now: `adb shell cmd connectivity airplane-mode enable; sleep 3; adb shell cmd connectivity airplane-mode disable; sleep 8`. Non-destructive; ~12 seconds.
- **`input tap` lands in the wrong place / "let me try the actual field locations and type" mid-turn** → coordinate-space confusion: you read pixel positions from a scaled rendering of the screenshot instead of the file's native dimensions. See "Tap coordinate discipline" in the On-device interaction section. The fix is never to iterate-and-guess; it's to verify `adb shell wm size` (this AVD is `1080x2400`) and read coordinates from the PNG file directly. WebViews use the same absolute device-pixel coordinates as native UI — no browser-style translation needed.
- **`INSTALL_FAILED_INSUFFICIENT_STORAGE` on a ~128 MB APK** → see "Storage discipline" in the "Booting the emulator" section above. Short version: `adb uninstall` only recovers ~100 MB because the bloat is system (Play Services / dalvik cache), not your app. If the launcher is current (passes `-partition-size 16384`) and you still see this, the underlying `userdata-qemu.img` is on the old 6 GB layout — needs a one-time `-wipe-data` boot to actualize the larger partition. **Always preflight with `adb shell df -h /data` before installs ≥ 50 MB.**
- **Edge-to-edge layout regression on Android 15 / SDK 35+** → `BaseActivity.kt` has the inset listener and bleed-color hooks; check `shouldApplyDefaultSystemBarInsets()`, `statusBarBleedColor()`, `navigationBarBleedColor()`, and `onSystemBarInsetsChanged()` before adding ad-hoc per-Activity insets handling. See `docs/agentic-showcase-edge-to-edge-deepdive.md` for the full rationale.
